    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Maintaining children's motivation to learn effectively is a crucial topic in K-12 education to mitigate the decrease in learning motivation and dropout rates. One way to inspire motivation to learn is through immersion. We combine the immersive potential of augmented reality (AR), narrative, and large language models (LLMs) to bridge fantasy with reality in a mobile application. Moon Story teaches elementary schoolers astronomy and environmental science by building upon learning theories such as culturally-relevant pedagogy. Using our application, a child embarks on a journey inspired by Chinese mythology, engages in real-world AR activities, and converses with a fictional character powered by an LLM. We conducted a controlled experiment (N=50) with two conditions: one using an LLM and one that was hard-coded. Both conditions resulted in learning gains, high engagement levels, and increased science learning motivation. Participants in the LLM condition also wrote more relevant answers. Finally, participants of both East Asian and non-East Asian heritage found the culturally-based narrative compelling.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Guo, M. (2023). Scientific and Fantastical: Facilitate Science Learning and Knowledge Retention with Augmented Reality, Narratives, and Large Language Models. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/cc153kf7670. https://doi.org/10.25740/cc153kf7670.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
